{"source":"Rivers: Natural Earth 10m river centerlines (public domain), simplified. Mountain ranges: approximate lines drawn by hand.","states":[{"id":"ap","n":"Andhra Pradesh","cap":"Amaravati","ll":[16.51,80.52],"ty":"S","rg":"S","f":"Andhra Pradesh has the second longest coastline in India, after Gujarat.","cn":"Amaravati is being built as a new planned capital city."},{"id":"ar","n":"Arunachal Pradesh","cap":"Itanagar","ll":[27.08,93.61],"ty":"S","rg":"NE","f":"Its name means land of the dawn-lit mountains. Some of India's earliest sunrises are seen here.","q":1},{"id":"as","n":"Assam","cap":"Dispur","ll":[26.14,91.79],"ty":"S","rg":"NE","f":"Assam grows a lot of India's tea. Kaziranga National Park is home to most of the world's one-horned rhinos.","cn":"Dispur is a part of the city of Guwahati.","q":1},{"id":"br","n":"Bihar","cap":"Patna","ll":[25.59,85.14],"ty":"S","rg":"E","f":"Patna was once Pataliputra, capital of the Maurya Empire. Ancient Nalanda University was in Bihar.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"cg","n":"Chhattisgarh","cap":"Raipur","ll":[21.25,81.63],"ty":"S","rg":"C","f":"Chitrakote Falls on the Indravati river is called the Niagara of India.","q":1},{"id":"ga","n":"Goa","cap":"Panaji","ll":[15.49,73.83],"ty":"S","rg":"W","f":"Goa is India's smallest state by area.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"gj","n":"Gujarat","cap":"Gandhinagar","ll":[23.22,72.65],"ty":"S","rg":"W","f":"Gujarat has India's longest coastline. Gir forest is the only wild home of Asiatic lions.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"hr","n":"Haryana","cap":"Chandigarh","ll":[30.73,76.78],"ty":"S","rg":"N","f":"Three famous Battles of Panipat were fought on the plains of Haryana.","cn":"Chandigarh is shared with Punjab.","q":1},{"id":"hp","n":"Himachal Pradesh","cap":"Shimla","ll":[31.1,77.17],"ty":"S","rg":"N","f":"Shimla was the summer capital of British India.","cn":"Dharamshala is the winter capital.","q":1},{"id":"jh","n":"Jharkhand","cap":"Ranchi","ll":[23.34,85.31],"ty":"S","rg":"E","f":"Jharkhand is rich in minerals like coal and iron ore.","q":1},{"id":"ka","n":"Karnataka","cap":"Bengaluru","ll":[12.97,77.59],"ty":"S","rg":"S","f":"Bengaluru is a big centre for software and space science. ISRO has its headquarters there.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"kl","n":"Kerala","cap":"Thiruvananthapuram","ll":[8.52,76.94],"ty":"S","rg":"S","f":"The summer monsoon usually reaches Kerala first, around the start of June.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"mp","n":"Madhya Pradesh","cap":"Bhopal","ll":[23.26,77.41],"ty":"S","rg":"C","f":"Madhya Pradesh is called the heart of India. The Tropic of Cancer passes through it.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"mh","n":"Maharashtra","cap":"Mumbai","ll":[19.08,72.88],"ty":"S","rg":"W","f":"The Ajanta and Ellora caves, with ancient paintings and carvings, are in Maharashtra.","cn":"Nagpur is the winter capital.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"mn","n":"Manipur","cap":"Imphal","ll":[24.82,93.94],"ty":"S","rg":"NE","f":"Loktak Lake in Manipur has floating islands called phumdis.","q":1},{"id":"ml","n":"Meghalaya","cap":"Shillong","ll":[25.58,91.89],"ty":"S","rg":"NE","f":"Mawsynram and Cherrapunji in Meghalaya are among the wettest places on Earth.","q":1},{"id":"mz","n":"Mizoram","cap":"Aizawl","ll":[23.73,92.72],"ty":"S","rg":"NE","f":"Mizoram means land of the hill people.","q":1},{"id":"nl","n":"Nagaland","cap":"Kohima","ll":[25.67,94.11],"ty":"S","rg":"NE","f":"The Hornbill Festival celebrates the cultures of Nagaland's many tribes.","q":1},{"id":"od","n":"Odisha","cap":"Bhubaneswar","ll":[20.3,85.82],"ty":"S","rg":"E","f":"The Konark Sun Temple is shaped like a giant chariot with 24 carved stone wheels.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"pb","n":"Punjab","cap":"Chandigarh","ll":[30.73,76.78],"ty":"S","rg":"N","f":"Punjab means land of five rivers. The Golden Temple is in Amritsar.","cn":"Chandigarh is shared with Haryana.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"rj","n":"Rajasthan","cap":"Jaipur","ll":[26.91,75.79],"ty":"S","rg":"W","f":"Rajasthan is India's largest state by area. The Thar Desert covers much of it.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"sk","n":"Sikkim","cap":"Gangtok","ll":[27.33,88.61],"ty":"S","rg":"NE","f":"Sikkim was the first Indian state to switch fully to organic farming.","q":1},{"id":"tn","n":"Tamil Nadu","cap":"Chennai","ll":[13.08,80.27],"ty":"S","rg":"S","f":"The Brihadeeswarar Temple in Thanjavur was completed in 1010 CE.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"tg","n":"Telangana","cap":"Hyderabad","ll":[17.39,78.49],"ty":"S","rg":"S","f":"Telangana became a separate state in 2014. Hyderabad's Charminar was built in 1591.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"tr","n":"Tripura","cap":"Agartala","ll":[23.83,91.29],"ty":"S","rg":"NE","f":"Tripura is surrounded by Bangladesh on three sides.","q":1},{"id":"up","n":"Uttar Pradesh","cap":"Lucknow","ll":[26.85,80.95],"ty":"S","rg":"N","f":"The Taj Mahal is in Agra, and the Ganga and Yamuna meet at Prayagraj.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"uk","n":"Uttarakhand","cap":"Dehradun","ll":[30.32,78.03],"ty":"S","rg":"N","f":"The Ganga and the Yamuna both begin at glaciers in Uttarakhand.","cn":"Gairsain (Bhararisain) is the summer capital.","q":1},{"id":"wb","n":"West Bengal","cap":"Kolkata","ll":[22.57,88.36],"ty":"S","rg":"E","f":"Kolkata was the capital of British India until 1911.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"an","n":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands","cap":"Sri Vijaya Puram","ll":[11.62,92.73],"ty":"UT","rg":"S","f":"Indira Point, the southernmost point of India, is on Great Nicobar Island.","cn":"The capital was called Port Blair until 2024."},{"id":"ch","n":"Chandigarh","cap":"Chandigarh","ll":[30.73,76.78],"ty":"UT","rg":"N","f":"Chandigarh was planned by the architect Le Corbusier. It is the capital of both Punjab and Haryana."},{"id":"dd","n":"Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu","cap":"Daman","ll":[20.4,72.83],"ty":"UT","rg":"W","f":"Two union territories joined into one in 2020."},{"id":"dl","n":"Delhi","cap":"New Delhi","ll":[28.61,77.21],"ty":"UT","rg":"N","f":"Every Independence Day, the national flag is raised at the Red Fort in Delhi.","q":1,"k":1},{"id":"jk","n":"Jammu and Kashmir","cap":"Srinagar","ll":[34.08,74.8],"ty":"UT","rg":"N","f":"Dal Lake in Srinagar is famous for its houseboats and shikaras.","cn":"Srinagar is the summer capital and Jammu is the winter capital."},{"id":"la","n":"Ladakh","cap":"Leh","ll":[34.15,77.58],"ty":"UT","rg":"N","f":"Ladakh is a high, cold desert surrounded by mountains."},{"id":"ld","n":"Lakshadweep","cap":"Kavaratti","ll":[10.57,72.64],"ty":"UT","rg":"S","f":"Lakshadweep is a group of 36 small coral islands in the Arabian Sea.","q":1},{"id":"py","n":"Puducherry","cap":"Puducherry","ll":[11.94,79.81],"ty":"UT","rg":"S","f":"Puducherry was ruled by France until 1954, and parts of the old town still look French.","q":1}],"rivers":[{"id":"ganga","n":"Ganga","len":2525,"src":"Gangotri Glacier, Uttarakhand","mouth":"Bay of Bengal","f":"India's national river. With the Brahmaputra it builds the Sundarbans delta, the largest delta on Earth.","c":"#5ff0ff","lines":[[[79.84,30.89],[79.81,30.82],[79.87,30.69],[79.72,30.51],[79.55,30.57],[79.32,30.4],[79.27,30.3],[78.7,30.24],[78.59,30.15],[78.59,30.07],[78.46,30.07],[78.32,30.14],[78.15,29.93],[78.19,29.71],[77.99,29.57],[78.12,29.25],[78.08,29.14],[78.09,28.84],[78.26,28.49],[78.29,28.31],[78.46,28.11],[78.63,27.99],[78.9,27.91],[78.98,27.83],[79.16,27.82],[79.2,27.69],[79.41,27.65],[79.55,27.54],[79.7,27.24],[79.84,27.15],[79.93,27.14],[80.11,26.87],[80.14,26.74],[80.25,26.68],[80.35,26.49],[80.53,26.36],[80.56,26.24],[80.67,26.11],[80.75,26.11],[80.81,26.03],[81.03,26.06],[81.07,26.0],[81.2,25.94],[81.22,25.87],[81.39,25.77],[81.4,25.67],[81.63,25.59],[81.7,25.49],[81.91,25.51],[81.9,25.41],[81.97,25.32],[82.04,25.34],[82.11,25.28],[82.21,25.3],[82.2,25.19],[82.27,25.19],[82.23,25.22],[82.29,25.28],[82.33,25.22],[82.4,25.26],[82.55,25.16],[82.59,25.17],[82.57,25.22],[82.64,25.22],[82.84,25.12],[83.01,25.21],[83.04,25.32],[83.17,25.35],[83.18,25.41],[83.12,25.45],[83.19,25.53],[83.41,25.49],[83.53,25.4],[83.49,25.52],[83.62,25.6],[83.71,25.61],[83.77,